 Linux Basics

🔹 Users & Groups
Q1. How do you create a new user and assign them a home directory and default shell?
sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash john
sudo passwd john

-m → create home directory

-s → specify shell

Q2. What’s the difference between /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow?
/etc/passwd → stores user account info (username, UID, GID, home dir, shell).

/etc/shadow → stores encrypted passwords and password expiry details.

/etc/shadow is readable only by root for security.

Q3. How do you switch to another user without logging out?
su - john
sudo -u john -i

Q4. How do you check the groups a user belongs to?
groups john
id john

Q5. How do you lock/unlock a user account?
sudo usermod -L john   # Lock
sudo usermod -U john   # Unlock

Q6. What happens to files when a user is deleted?
sudo userdel john

Files remain on the system with old UID ownership.

To delete home directory as well:

sudo userdel -r john

Q7. What’s the difference between su and sudo?
su → Switch user (needs target user password).

sudo → Run commands as root or another user (needs your password, configured in /etc/sudoers).

🔹 Permissions
Q8. Explain the meaning of -rwxr-xr--.
- → file type (regular file).

rwx → owner has read, write, execute.

r-x → group has read, execute.

r-- → others have read only.

Q9. What is the difference between absolute (numeric) and symbolic permissions?
Numeric → chmod 755 file

Symbolic → chmod u=rwx,g=rx,o=r file

Both give same result.
Q10. How do you change ownership of a file or directory?
sudo chown john:developers file.txt

Q11. What’s the difference between chmod 777 and chmod 755?
777 → everyone can read, write, execute (least secure).

755 → owner can read/write/execute, others can only read + execute (common for scripts/web files).

Q12. What are SUID, SGID, and Sticky Bit?
SUID (chmod 4755 file) → file runs with owner’s privileges. Example: /usr/bin/passwd.

SGID (chmod 2755 dir) → new files inherit group ownership.

Sticky Bit (chmod 1777 dir) → only file owner can delete (used in /tmp).

Q13. How do you give only execute permission to the owner of a file?
chmod u+x file.sh

Q14. A file has rw-rw-r-- but the user still cannot edit it. Why?
 Possible reasons:
The user is not owner or in the group.

File system is mounted read-only.

ACLs (Access Control Lists) override normal permissions (getfacl file.txt).
